## Getting started

Create your environment file first (the app reads secrets from it):

```bash
cp .env.example .env
# Windows PowerShell: Copy-Item .env.example .env
```

Then edit `.env` and set `SECRET_KEY` (and `POSTGRES_PASSWORD` if you use
PostgreSQL).

### With Docker (recommended)

```bash
docker compose up --build
```

The app will be available at <http://localhost:{{ port }}>.

### Local development

```bash
python -m venv .venv
source .venv/bin/activate   # Windows: .venv\Scripts\activate
pip install -r requirements-dev.txt
{%- if include_precommit %}
pre-commit install
{%- endif %}

python backend/manage.py migrate
python backend/manage.py runserver
```

Create an admin user:

```bash
python backend/manage.py createsuperuser
```

Then open <http://localhost:8000/admin/>.

## Make targets

If you have GNU make installed (Git Bash or WSL on Windows):

```bash
make help        # list all targets
make up          # docker compose up -d --build
make migrate     # apply migrations
make superuser   # create an admin user
make test        # run pytest
make lint        # ruff + black check
make fmt         # auto-fix
```

## Tests and linting

```bash
pytest
{%- if include_precommit %}

ruff check .
black --check .
{%- endif %}
```

## Accessing from another machine

Django only accepts hosts listed in `ALLOWED_HOSTS`. To reach the app from
another device on your network, add your LAN IP or hostname to the
`ALLOWED_HOSTS` line in `.env`, for example:

```
ALLOWED_HOSTS=localhost,127.0.0.1,192.168.1.50
```

